{"parentUuid":"308412da-dc34-4f23-a515-f83cc3f8a0bc","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"edited_text_file","filename":"C:\\Users\\nsdha\\OneDrive\\binary16\\prime-silo\\app\\L0\\_all\\mod\\_prime_silo\\widgets\\force_graph_2d\\index.js","snippet":"1\t// Organic 2D force-graph drop-in renderer.\n2\t//\n3\t// Slots into `memoray.lineage_graph`, `kg3d.synoptic_web`, and\n4\t// `codegraph.canvas` via the same `options.renderer = { mount, update,\n5\t// dispose }` hook that `three_renderer` uses. The widget cores stay\n6\t// dependency-free (default 2D SVG); callers that want the living, organic\n7\t// graph — the Memo-Ray look (force-simulated motion, node glow, animated\n8\t// trace particles on the active path, a corner minimap) — pass an instance\n9\t// returned by `createForceGraph2DRenderer()` and the widget never learns the\n10\t// graphing library exists.\n11\t//\n12\t// ## Offline by design\n13\t//\n14\t// Unlike `three_renderer` (which lazy-loads `3d-force-graph` from a CDN), the\n15\t// 2D library is **vendored locally** at `vendor/force-graph.module.js` (a\n16\t// self-contained esbuild bundle of vasturiano/force-graph with every d3 /\n17\t// lodash dependency inlined — zero bare imports, see vendor/README.md). The\n18\t// default loader dynamic-imports that local path, so the renderer works with\n19\t// no network at all. The import is still lazy (only on first mount) so pages\n20\t// that never open a graph pay nothing.\n21\t//\n22\t// Tests inject `options.loader = () => Promise<ForceGraph>` so the node runner\n23\t// never touches the vendored bundle or the DOM.\n24\t//\n25\t// ## Layout contract (shared with three_renderer)\n26\t//\n27\t//   • `memoray.lineage_graph` → positions carry { x, y, depth, node }\n28\t//   • `kg3d.synoptic_web`     → positions carry { x, y, radius, layer, color, node }\n29\t//   • `codegraph.canvas`      → positions carry { x, y, radius, type, color, node }\n30\t//\n31\t// `layoutToGraphData(layout, physics)` normalises a